# Per-project development domains, so each project gets its own cookie jar.
#
# Five things here are load-bearing:
#
#   http://   Without the scheme Caddy treats the site as HTTPS, issues a
#             certificate from its internal CA and asks to be trusted. Nothing
#             on loopback gains anything from that.
#   Host      Caddy forwards the original Host header by default, unlike nginx.
#             That is what satisfies Vite, whose allowedHosts permits localhost
#             and *.localhost and nothing else. Do not override it.
#   websocket reverse_proxy upgrades connections with no extra directive, which
#             is what lets Vite HMR reconnect through the proxy.
#   { ... }   A site block cannot be written on one line. Caddy needs the
#             opening brace at end of line and the closing brace on its own, or
#             it reports "Unexpected '}' because no matching opening brace" and
#             refuses to start at all.
#   upstream  Both loopback families are listed, because a dev server may bind
#             [::1] only, as Vite and Next both may, and an IPv4-only upstream
#             then answers 502 while the server is plainly up on the port.
#             `lb_policy first` is what makes the pair a failover instead of a
#             round robin: the default selection is random, so without it half
#             the requests would go to whichever address nothing is holding.
#             Never 0.0.0.0 here. That is a bind address meaning every
#             interface, and dialing it times out rather than being refused,
#             so a request stalls instead of failing. A server bound to it is
#             reached through 127.0.0.1 anyway, loopback being one of them.
#
# Browsers resolve *.localhost to 127.0.0.1 themselves and never read
# /etc/hosts, so none of these need an entry there. curl does. See README.md.

# Covers a site added without the http:// prefix above: without this, Caddy
# would still reach for a certificate from its internal CA for that one.
{
	auto_https off
}

http://outdoor-maps.localhost {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001 [::1]:3001 {
		lb_policy first
		fail_duration 5s
	}
}
